A cutting-edge approach has been developed to accurately identify genuine hadronic tau decays in the CMS detector, distinguishing them from those originating from quarks, gluons, electrons, and muons. By incorporating properties of reconstructed hadronic tau decays into a novel classification model, a deep neural network achieves enhanced classification performance in conjunction with previously developed methods. Using data collected by the CMS detector at 13 TeV during the 2016 LHC proton-proton collision campaign, this approach demonstrates improved efficiency in distinguishing hadronic tau decays, achieving an approximate 20% improvement over traditional methods. Additionally, this technique is applied to real-world scenarios, including computations of tau decay rates and searches for the Higgs boson's interaction with tau leptons. To ensure validity, this approach's performance is thoroughly verified through independent techniques. Its applications are also described, allowing this method to be incorporated into CMS analyses. Furthermore, this technique serves as a model for addressing detection and reconstruction challenges in novel, particle physics-based methods. An accompanying source code is available for public use. Additionally, this approach's performance is compared to equivalent techniques employed by the ATLAS collaboration. This study is written by a team of authors to benefit the scientific community, aiming to facilitate adoption in a variety of particle physics analyses. Importantly, this technique addresses detection and reconstruction challenges applicable to multiple detector models, making it a valuable asset to the scientific community. To facilitate adoption, accompanying software is provided for public use, utilizing a framework previously introduced in a different study. Moreover, performance comparisons with a competing collaboration are provided to foster collaboration and knowledge-sharing among researchers, utilizing a format previously established in other publications. Ultimately, to gain widespread adoption, this technique is recommended for incorporation into two specific CMS analyses. 

